# Multi-group X11 keymap injection: type in the group that is in force, never
# by switching it (apply to Selkies 2.0.0,
# 3ec56fb1538cf077c27156f5ab75b6595a83c461; re-derive when bumping the pin).
#
# The successor of the pre-2.0 keymap-injection.patch. 2.0 rewrote X11
# injection around XKB (x11_xkb.py: real group/level placement, group locks),
# which fixed the core-keymap guesswork that patch worked around — but it
# still types the wrong character whenever the ACTIVE input source is not the
# one it picks. GNOME with two input sources gives the keymap two XKB groups,
# and 2.0 places every keysym at the LOWEST group carrying it:
#
#   * a keysym the first group carries is injected under whatever group is
#     locked (_enter_group returns early for group 0). With Swedish active as
#     the second source of us,se, ':' lands on US Shift+Semicolon and types
#     the Swedish key there — 'Ö'; '>' -> ':', '|' -> '*', '{' -> 'Å'.
#   * a keysym only a later group carries is injected by locking that group
#     for the press and restoring it 0.5s later. GNOME owns the layout and
#     resets the lock within milliseconds, but the injector trusts its cached
#     lock for the rest of the linger window, so after the first key every
#     later one types under the base group: å ä ö -> å ' ; with US active.
#
# Measured on vm-gnome-selkies (GNOME 46, X11) with a real client typing
# ":<>|^@{;/[" (US-layout client) and "åäö" (Swedish-layout client), twice
# each, in every combination of source order and active source:
#
#                          upstream 2.0    this patch
#   us,se  US active       åäö wrong       OK
#   us,se  SE active       symbols wrong   OK
#   se,us  SE active       OK              OK
#   se,us  US active       both wrong      OK
#
# The fix, in _XTestKeyboard._placement: use the keysym's placement in the
# group in force (the user's lock, or this injector's while a hold lasts)
# when that group carries it; otherwise report no placement, which sends
# _resolve to 2.0's own overlay path — the keysym bound to a spare keycode
# with one group, which types the same under any lock. No group lock is ever
# taken, so the user's input source never moves and GNOME has nothing to
# fight. XkbLink gains a per-group index (locate_in_group) for the lookup,
# filled for all four groups through each key's out-of-range rule (wrap by
# default, clamp, redirect): a key declaring one group — XF86Copy, media
# keys — answers the same under any group, and indexing only the groups a key
# declares sent those to an overlay keycode, past anything that grabs the
# real one (whistler-copy-agent's XF86Copy/XF86Paste grab broke exactly so).
# Cost: one XkbGetState round trip per key press on a local X server.
